<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Programmatic Demo | <%= appInfo.name %></title>
    <style>
        :root {
            --bg: linear-gradient(135deg, #efe6dd 0%, #d9ecf0 100%);
            --panel: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);
            --border: #d7c6ba;
            --accent: #0c7489;
            --accent-soft: #dbf0f5;
            --text: #24313a;
            --muted: #62707a;
        }

        * { box-sizing: border-box; }
        body {
            margin: 0;
            font-family: "Trebuchet MS", "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
            background: var(--bg);
            color: var(--text);
            min-height: 100vh;
        }
        a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
        .page {
            width: min(1180px, calc(100% - 32px));
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 32px 0 48px;
        }
        .hero, .panel {
            background: var(--panel);
            border: 1px solid var(--border);
            border-radius: 28px;
            padding: 26px;
            box-shadow: 0 18px 40px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08);
        }
        .hero-top, .actions, .nav {
            display: flex;
            flex-wrap: wrap;
            gap: 12px;
            justify-content: space-between;
            align-items: flex-start;
        }
        .nav {
            justify-content: flex-start;
            margin-top: 18px;
        }
        .pill, .link-btn {
            display: inline-flex;
            align-items: center;
            min-height: 42px;
            padding: 0 14px;
            border-radius: 999px;
            border: 1px solid var(--border);
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72);
        }
        .pill {
            background: var(--accent-soft);
            color: var(--accent);
            font-weight: 700;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            letter-spacing: 0.04em;
            font-size: 0.84rem;
        }
        .link-btn.current, .link-btn.primary {
            background: var(--accent);
            color: white;
            border-color: var(--accent);
            font-weight: 700;
        }
        .grid {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
            gap: 18px;
            margin-top: 22px;
        }
        .mono {
            font-family: Consolas, "Courier New", monospace;
            font-size: 0.94rem;
            word-break: break-word;
        }
        p, li { color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.6; }
        pre {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 18px;
            border-radius: 18px;
            background: #172026;
            color: #f1f5f9;
            overflow: auto;
            white-space: pre-wrap;
        }
        ul {
            margin: 0;
            padding-left: 20px;
        }
        h3 {
            margin-top: 0;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="page">
        <section class="hero">
            <div class="hero-top">
                <div>
                    <span class="pill">Fetch Failure Demo</span>
                    <h1>Skip <span class="mono">/oauth2/authorize</span> and try to fetch Cognito <span class="mono">/login</span></h1>
                    <p>This page intentionally uses server-side <span class="mono">fetch</span> to call Cognito <span class="mono">/login</span>, then it tries to force a token request anyway. The point is to show, in code, why Cognito managed login does not turn into a supported server-only SSO login flow.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="actions">
                    <a class="link-btn primary" href="/programmatic-demo">Run Demo Again</a>
                </div>
            </div>

            <nav class="nav" aria-label="Route switcher">
                <% pageLinks.forEach((link) => { %>
                    <a class="link-btn <%= link.current ? 'current' : '' %>" href="<%= link.href %>"><%= link.label %></a>
                <% }) %>
            </nav>
        </section>

        <section class="grid">
            <article class="panel">
                <h2>High-level result</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Login URL:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= fetchDemo.loginUrl %></span></li>
                    <li><strong>Token URL:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= fetchDemo.tokenUrl %></span></li>
                    <li><strong>Callback location:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= fetchDemo.callbackLocation || 'none' %></span></li>
                    <li><strong>Authorization code found:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= fetchDemo.derivedAuthorizationCode || 'none' %></span></li>
                </ul>
            </article>

            <article class="panel">
                <h2>What this demonstrates</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>You can make raw HTTP calls to Cognito <span class="mono">/login</span> from your server.</li>
                    <li>That does not reproduce the browser-managed login flow that Cognito expects.</li>
                    <li>If no real browser callback returns a valid code, the token call fails or returns an error payload.</li>
                    <li>Even if Cognito returns cookies during these fetches, they stay in the server-side request context unless you build your own unsupported cookie orchestration.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
        </section>

        <% fetchDemo.steps.forEach((step) => { %>
            <section class="grid">
                <article class="panel">
                    <h2><%= step.name %></h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Method:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= step.request.method %></span></li>
                        <li><strong>URL:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= step.request.url %></span></li>
                        <% if (step.request.body) { %>
                            <li><strong>Body:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= step.request.body %></span></li>
                        <% } %>
                    </ul>
                </article>

                <article class="panel">
                    <h2>Response</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Status:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= step.result.statusCode %></span></li>
                        <li><strong>Content-Type:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= step.result.contentType %></span></li>
                        <li><strong>Location header:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= step.result.location || 'none' %></span></li>
                        <li><strong>Set-Cookie headers:</strong> <span class="mono"><%= step.result.setCookie.length %></span></li>
                    </ul>
                </article>
            </section>

            <section class="grid">
                <article class="panel">
                    <h3>Body preview</h3>
                    <pre><%= step.result.bodyPreview || 'No response body was captured.' %></pre>
                </article>

                <article class="panel">
                    <h3>Cookie summary</h3>
                    <% if (step.result.setCookie.length) { %>
                        <ul>
                            <% step.result.setCookie.forEach((cookieLine) => { %>
                                <li><span class="mono"><%= cookieLine %></span></li>
                            <% }) %>
                        </ul>
                    <% } else { %>
                        <p>No <span class="mono">Set-Cookie</span> headers were returned on this step.</p>
                    <% } %>
                </article>
            </section>
        <% }) %>

        <section class="grid">
            <article class="panel">
                <h2>Why the token step fails</h2>
                <p>The token endpoint expects a valid authorization code that came back through the browser redirect flow. In this demo, we intentionally never complete that user-driven flow, so the token request is forced to run without a real code or with a code that never reached the app through the supported callback path.</p>
            </article>

            <article class="panel">
                <h2>AWS boundary</h2>
                <p>This is useful as a demonstration, but it is not a supported Cognito managed-login authentication model. Cognito’s managed login pages are meant for browser interaction, not for replacing the interactive flow with server-side fetch calls.</p>
            </article>
        </section>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
